---
/**
 * How a place page's insights are made, from the figure that stands out to the grading
 * that decides whether a run is published. Every sentence here has to stay true of
 * insights/src/: detect.ts, propose.ts, vocabulary.ts, links.ts, falsify.ts, run.ts,
 * grade.ts, mutate.ts and score.ts. The grading numbers come from insights/metrics.json
 * when it exists, and the mirror of this page under /fr/ says the same in French.
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "../../i18n/ui";
import { numbers, percent } from "../../lib/format";
import { metrics } from "../../lib/insights";

const locale = "en";

const toc = [
  { id: "stands-out", label: "What stands out" },
  { id: "proposed", label: "The reasons proposed" },
  { id: "premise", label: "The premise" },
  { id: "link", label: "The link" },
  { id: "adversary", label: "The adversary" },
  { id: "grading", label: "Grading" },
  { id: "sources", label: "What it draws on" },
];

const n = numbers(locale);
const whole = (fraction: number) => percent(locale, fraction * 100, { digits: 0 });
const on = (iso: string) =>
  new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-GB", { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" }).format(new Date(iso));

const published = metrics && metrics.published.graded > 0 ? metrics.published : null;
const agreement = metrics?.agreement ?? null;
const planted = metrics && metrics.planted.total > 0 ? metrics.planted : null;
const KINDS: [string, string][] = [
  ["unit", "The place swapped for a commune picked at random"],
  ["year", "The other census year read"],
  ["direction", "The comparison turned round"],
  ["number", "The threshold halved, or raised by 20"],
  ["field", "The measure swapped for one from another topic"],
];
const kinds = planted ? KINDS.filter(([kind]) => (planted.byKind[kind]?.total ?? 0) > 0) : [];

const file = (path: string) => `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${path}`;
---

<Docs
  locale={locale}
  route="docs/insights/"
  title="How the insights are made"
  description="How a figure that stands out gets possible reasons on a place page, and how each one is checked."
  lede="A place page can show a figure that stands out, with possible reasons a language model proposed for it. This is how the figures are picked and how each reason is checked before it’s shown."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="stands-out">
    <h2>What stands out</h2>
    <p>
      A figure can stand out in 3 ways. A commune of 5,000 people or more is in the top or bottom 1% of those
      communes on a measure. A unit’s figure moved since 2014 by 3 standard deviations or more from the average
      change across its level, for a level of 10 units or more and a measure the 2014 census asked the same way. Or
      a commune’s or a province’s gap from its province’s or région’s figure is 3 standard deviations or more from
      the average gap under that parent, where the parent holds 8 units or more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every measure is a rate, a share or an average, since a raw count would pick out a big place for being big.
      A commune matched to 2014 through the crosswalk has no change to compare, since its 2014 figure covers other
      ground. The 300 figures that stand out most are kept, at most 3 for any one place.
    </p>
    <p>
      The languages people speak, read and write, education and illiteracy move slowly. When one of them swings in a
      place whose population moved by less than 10%, and no neighbour moved the same way by at least half as much,
      the figure is flagged as a possible error in the data. It’s shown with that flag alone, and no reasons are
      proposed for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="proposed">
    <h2>The reasons proposed</h2>
    <p>
      Each other figure goes to a language model, with every measure for the place, its province or région, its
      neighbours and Morocco, and the model is asked for 2 to 4 hypotheses. A hypothesis has a claim, the possible
      reason in a few words; a premise, a fact about the place that a data test can check; and a link, why that
      fact would explain the figure.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same question is asked 5 times. The answers are merged by what their data tests check, and a
      hypothesis’s support is how many of the 5 proposed it. How much the 5 disagree is recorded for each figure as
      its semantic entropy, taken over those merged groups. A word list then drops any hypothesis that names a
      person, an ethnic or religious group or a political actor, in either language.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="premise">
    <h2>The premise</h2>
    <p>
      A premise comes with a data test, written in a vocabulary of 3 checks: one figure against another or
      against a number, how far a figure moved since 2014, and where a place ranks in its province, its région or
      Morocco. The test reads the census figures and arithmetic decides it. It comes out true or false, and the
      numbers it read are kept beside it, so anyone can check the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      A test that uses the figure’s own measure, or another part of the same whole, such as a different age band
      when the figure is an age band, is refused, and its hypothesis with it: a premise has to be a different fact
      from the figure it explains. A missing figure fails the test. Only a premise whose test passed goes on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="link">
    <h2>The link</h2>
    <p>
      The link is a claim about cause, and the census alone can’t settle it. Where a link test fits, the model
      proposes one, and it runs across every place of that level. One kind ranks the places on the premise’s
      measure and on the figure’s, in 2024 or by how each changed since 2014, and reads how closely the 2 rankings
      go together, with a p-value from 999 shuffles. The other splits each région’s places at their median on the
      premise’s measure and compares the figure’s median between the 2 halves, with a Mann-Whitney test.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each test runs again with 3 measures from unrelated topics in place of the premise’s, as placebos, and the
      premise has to go with the figure more strongly than all 3 do. The p-values of every link test in a run are
      corrected together, with the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure at a false discovery rate of 5%.
    </p>
    <p>
      A link that passes, beats its placebos and goes the way it claimed is shown as consistent with the pattern
      across places. That’s a pattern the premise and the figure share, which is weaker than one causing the other.
      A link test that fails drops its hypothesis. Where no link test fits, or one can’t be run, as with fewer than
      30 places to compare, the link is shown as proposed, not tested.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="adversary">
    <h2>The adversary</h2>
    <p>
      A hypothesis whose premise passed goes to a second language model, a different one from the model that
      proposed it. It sees the same figures and the hypothesis, and tries to break it with a counter-test in the
      same vocabulary, one that would come out true if the premise didn’t explain the figure. The counter-test is
      run the same way, and one that comes out true drops the hypothesis. The adversary can also decline to argue
      with a hypothesis that names a person, a group or a political actor, which drops it too.
    </p>
    <p>
      A counter-test that fails or is refused leaves the hypothesis standing, and so does an answer without one.
      Each figure then shows up to 3 of the hypotheses left, those with the most support first. A figure with none
      left is kept off the page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="grading">
    <h2>Grading</h2>
    <p>
      A person grades a sample of the latest run by hand: up to 50 hypotheses that passed every check and 50 that
      stopped somewhere, at most one of each from a figure, shuffled together. The grader sees the figure, the
      claim, the premise with its numbers, the link and its test’s numbers, and nothing that says whether the
      hypothesis passed or where it stopped. The answer is yes or no: whether the hypothesis holds up. Up to 25 of
      the answers are graded again, blind, and Cohen’s kappa measures how far the 2 passes agree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every published hypothesis graded yes then has its data test corrupted in each of the 5 ways that fit it, and
      run again: the place swapped for a commune picked at random, the other census year read, the comparison
      turned round, the threshold halved or raised by 20, and the measure swapped for one from another topic. A
      planted error is caught when the corrupted test no longer passes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The share of published hypotheses graded yes gets a 95% Wilson interval. Nothing is published while the lower
      end of that interval, taken one-sided, is under 80%, while no planted errors were measured, or while they’re
      caught less often than at the last grading kept in the repository.
    </p>
    {metrics ? (
      <>
        {published && (
          <p>
            {n.format(published.yes)} of {n.format(published.graded)} published hypotheses were graded yes, so between{" "}
            {whole(published.low)} and {whole(published.high)} of what’s published holds up, with 95% confidence. The
            one-sided lower end the guard reads is {whole(published.lowOneSided)}.
          </p>
        )}
        {agreement && (
          <p>
            Over the {n.format(agreement.n)} answers graded a second time, the 2 passes agree at a Cohen’s kappa of{" "}
            {numbers(locale, 2, true).format(agreement.kappa)}.
          </p>
        )}
        {planted && (
          <>
            <p>{n.format(planted.caught)} of {n.format(planted.total)} planted errors were caught.</p>
            <div class="scroll" tabindex="0">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>The error planted</th><th class="num">Caught</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {kinds.map(([kind, label]) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{label}</td>
                      <td class="num">{n.format(planted.byKind[kind]!.caught)} of {n.format(planted.byKind[kind]!.total)}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </>
        )}
        <p class="note">These numbers are from {on(metrics.measuredAt)}.</p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p class="note">The numbers appear here after the first grading.</p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section id="sources">
    <h2>What it draws on</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09858">POPPER</a> (Huang et al., ICML 2025) has a language model design
      experiments that would falsify a hypothesis, and runs them with the error rate kept in check. The adversary’s
      counter-tests follow that idea.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.12/">Literature Meets Data</a> (Liu et al., ACL 2025) has a
      language model propose hypotheses from what’s already known about a subject together with the data at hand.
      The model here proposes from what it knows about places, with the census figures in front of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07421-0">Semantic entropy</a> (Farquhar et al., Nature,
      2024) measures how far a model’s repeated answers disagree in meaning. The discrete form, over groups of
      answers, is what’s recorded for each figure, with a data test standing for a meaning.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pipeline is in <a href={file("insights/")}><code>insights/</code></a>, and what it publishes is in{" "}
      <a href={file("data/v1/insights/")}><code>data/v1/insights/</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>

<style>
  .doc :global(#grading td.num),
  .doc :global(#grading th.num) {
    text-align: end;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
